
/* per chapter: [scene, [right idea, three other ideas], why] */
var EC={
e1:[["You have ₹500 and one free Saturday evening. You choose a concert ticket over a new bat grip and a film.",["Opportunity cost","Diminishing marginal utility","Inflation","Disguised unemployment"],"The real cost of the concert is the next best thing you gave up. Every choice has one."],
 ["A music school has ten pianos and forty students who all want daily practice. It must decide who plays, when and for how long.",["Scarcity and choice","Economic growth","Price index","Privatisation"],"Wants are more than resources, so someone has to choose how to allocate them. That is the central economic problem."]],
e2:[["A town gets a new mall and incomes rise, but the government school still has no toilets and the clinic has no doctor.",["Growth without development","Sustainable development","Disguised unemployment","Globalisation"],"Income rose, which is growth. Development also needs better health, education and living conditions."],
 ["Two states have the same income per head. In one, children stay in school longer and people live ten years more.",["Human Development Index","Per capita income","Poverty line","Index of industrial production"],"HDI adds health and education to income, so it separates the two states where income alone cannot."]],
e3:[["Your grandparents waited years for a scooter and a telephone line. You order a foreign-brand phone online and it arrives tomorrow.",["Liberalisation and globalisation","Nationalisation","Import substitution","Central planning"],"After 1991, licences were removed and the economy opened to foreign goods and investment."],
 ["The government sells its airline to a private business group.",["Privatisation","Liberalisation","Globalisation","Nationalisation"],"Transferring ownership of a public enterprise to the private sector is privatisation, done through disinvestment."]],
e4:[["Five family members work on one small plot. When two leave for the city, the harvest stays exactly the same.",["Disguised unemployment","Seasonal unemployment","Frictional unemployment","Relative poverty"],"The two extra workers added nothing to output. Their marginal product was zero, though they looked employed."],
 ["A vegetable seller borrows ₹1,000 from a moneylender each morning and repays ₹1,100 by night.",["Non-institutional credit","Institutional credit","Human capital formation","Agricultural marketing"],"Moneylenders are informal sources charging very high interest. Banks, cooperatives and self-help groups are the institutional alternative."]],
e5:[["A village cuts only as many trees each year as it plants, so that its children will still have a forest.",["Sustainable development","Economic growth","Privatisation","Globalisation"],"Meeting today’s needs without reducing what future generations can have is the Brundtland definition of sustainable development."],
 ["A city replaces diesel buses with CNG and electric ones, and solar panels appear on school roofs.",["Cleaner and non-conventional energy","Carrying capacity","Resource depletion","Land degradation"],"Switching to cleaner fuels and renewable sources is one of the main strategies for sustainable development."]],
e6:[["To find the school’s favourite music app, you ask 40 students picked by lottery from the register, not all 2,000.",["Random sampling","Census method","Secondary data","Tabulation"],"Every student had an equal chance of selection, so a small group can represent the whole."],
 ["For a project you copy rainfall figures from a government website instead of measuring the rain yourself.",["Secondary data","Primary data","Sample survey","Classification"],"Data collected by someone else for another purpose is secondary. It is cheaper but must be checked for reliability."]],
e7:[["A batter scores 0, 2, 5, 8 and 150. The commentator says he “averages 33”, which looks like none of his innings.",["The mean is distorted by an extreme value","The mode is the highest score","The range is zero","The correlation is negative"],"One huge score pulls the mean up. The median, 5, describes his usual innings better."],
 ["Two batters both average 40. One always scores between 35 and 45. The other gets either 0 or 80.",["Dispersion","Central tendency","Correlation","Index number"],"The averages are equal but the spread is not. Standard deviation measures that consistency."]],
e8:[["On hot days the ice-cream stall near school sells more. On cold days it sells less.",["Positive correlation","Negative correlation","No correlation","Standard deviation"],"Both variables move in the same direction, so the correlation is positive."],
 ["A news report says prices are 6% higher than a year ago. Someone tracked the cost of one fixed basket of goods to say that.",["Consumer price index","Median","Random sampling","Lorenz curve"],"An index number compares the cost of the same basket in the current year with a base year."]],
p1:[["Your housing society has members, a boundary wall, an elected committee and rules. Yet the police can enter and a court can overrule it.",["It lacks sovereignty","It lacks population","It lacks territory","It lacks government"],"It has three elements of a state but not the fourth. Sovereignty is supreme power, with nothing above it."],
 ["The ruling party loses an election and a new Prime Minister takes over. The country, its borders and its laws carry on.",["Governments change, the state continues","The nation has dissolved","Sovereignty has moved abroad","Society has become the state"],"Government is only the agency of the state. The state itself is permanent."]],
p2:[["Students in a hostel, tired of stolen chargers and fights, agree on rules and choose a warden to enforce them.",["Social contract","Divine origin","Force theory","Pluralism"],"People give up some freedom by agreement to get order and security: the core of Hobbes, Locke and Rousseau."],
 ["A class decides on a picnic to the hills. Those who wanted the beach go along willingly, because the decision was made for the good of the whole group.",["General will (Rousseau)","Absolute sovereign (Hobbes)","Divine right of kings","Natural rights (Locke)"],"For Rousseau the general will is the common good that each member accepts as their own. It is more than a count of votes."]],
p3:[["You can post an opinion criticising the government, choose your own career and open a shop without anyone’s permission.",["Liberalism","Communism","Divine right","Absolute sovereignty"],"Liberalism puts individual freedom first and limits what the state may do."],
 ["In a factory town the workers who make the shoes cannot afford them, while the owner’s profit keeps rising.",["Surplus value and class conflict (Marx)","Negative liberty","Popular sovereignty","Rule of law"],"Marx argued that owners keep the surplus value created by workers, which sets the two classes against each other."]],
p4:[["A powerful foreign country asks India to change one of its laws. Parliament ignores the request.",["External sovereignty","Pluralism","De facto sovereignty","Divine origin"],"A sovereign state is independent of outside control in making its decisions."],
 ["After a coup, a general in the capital gives all the orders, though the constitution still names the elected president.",["De facto sovereignty","De jure sovereignty","Popular sovereignty","External sovereignty"],"The general holds power in fact (de facto). The president holds it in law (de jure)."]],
p5:[["A judge decides a new kind of online fraud case, and other courts follow that ruling afterwards.",["Judicial precedent","Custom","Legislation","Equity"],"Decisions of higher courts become a source of law for later cases."],
 ["You must stop at a red light even on an empty road at midnight, or pay a fine.",["Law is backed by the state’s sanction","A custom of the community","A moral rule only","The general will"],"What separates law from custom or morality is that the state enforces it with a penalty."]],
p6:[["Nobody stops you from applying to a university abroad. Without money for the fees, that freedom means little.",["Negative liberty without positive liberty","Political equality","Legal justice","Popular sovereignty"],"Absence of restraint is negative liberty. Positive liberty needs the conditions that let you actually use the freedom."],
 ["Two siblings share a cake. The one who cuts agrees that the other chooses first.",["Justice as fairness (Rawls)","Negative liberty","Natural rights","Divine origin"],"Not knowing which slice you will get is like Rawls’s veil of ignorance. It pushes you to make the rule fair to everyone."],
 ["A scholarship pays the fees of a talented student whose family cannot afford the school.",["Equality of opportunity","Identical treatment for all","Negative liberty","Legal sovereignty"],"Equality does not mean treating everyone the same. It means giving everyone a fair chance to develop."]],
p7:[["Around the world people watch Hollywood films, wear jeans, use American apps and pay for oil in dollars.",["Hegemony as soft power","Hegemony as military power","Bipolarity","Non-alignment"],"US dominance works through culture and attraction as well as force. That is the soft or cultural side of hegemony."],
 ["A class has two rival groups. One breaks up, and the other now decides everything from the seating to the playlist.",["A unipolar world","A bipolar world","A multipolar world","Non-alignment"],"When the USSR collapsed in 1991, the USA was left as the only superpower."]],
p8:[["A student from France studies in Germany, works in Spain and pays in the same currency everywhere, without a visa.",["The European Union","ASEAN","SAARC","BRICS"],"The EU has a single market, free movement of people and a common currency, the euro, for most members."],
 ["Neighbours on a street agree to share a generator and a guard, but two houses that keep quarrelling block every decision.",["SAARC","The European Union","ASEAN","QUAD"],"South Asian cooperation has been held back by the rivalry between India and Pakistan."]],
p9:[["For decades, even through wars, India and Pakistan shared the waters of six rivers under a treaty signed in 1960.",["Indus Waters Treaty","Simla Agreement","Panchsheel","Tashkent Declaration"],"The World Bank brokered it in 1960 and it survived three wars. India put it on hold in 2025."],
 ["A river flows from India into Bangladesh, and farmers on both sides want its water in the dry season.",["River water sharing with Bangladesh","The Line of Actual Control","The Kashmir dispute","Panchsheel"],"Sharing the Ganga and the Teesta is a central issue between India and Bangladesh."]],
s1:[["You notice that students sit in the same friend groups at lunch every day, and you start asking why groups form that way.",["Sociology studies patterns in social relationships","Psychology studies the individual mind","Economics studies prices","History records past events"],"Looking for the pattern behind group behaviour, and not at one person, is the sociological way of seeing."],
 ["When factories opened, families left their villages, lived among strangers and worked by the clock. Thinkers wanted a science to explain this new society.",["The Industrial Revolution gave rise to sociology","The Green Revolution","Sanskritisation","Socialisation"],"Sociology emerged in 19th-century Europe to understand the changes brought by industry and the French Revolution."]],
s2:[["A grandmother says a demon swallows the sun in an eclipse. The school textbook explains the moon’s shadow.",["Comte’s law of three stages","Marx’s class struggle","Weber’s types of authority","Durkheim’s solidarity"],"Comte said human thought moves from the theological stage through the metaphysical to the positive, or scientific, stage."],
 ["Drivers obey a traffic constable because of the uniform and the law, not because they know the person.",["Legal-rational authority","Charismatic authority","Traditional authority","Mechanical solidarity"],"Weber’s legal-rational authority belongs to the office and its rules, not to the individual."]],
s3:[["Your family WhatsApp group is small, close and for life. The 300-member alumni group is large and formal.",["Primary and secondary groups","In-group and out-group","Ascribed and achieved status","Folkways and mores"],"Cooley’s primary group is intimate and face-to-face. Secondary groups are larger, impersonal and built around a purpose."],
 ["Being the eldest child was fixed at birth. Becoming team captain took years of effort.",["Ascribed and achieved status","Role conflict","Primary and secondary groups","Community and association"],"Ascribed status is given at birth. Achieved status is earned."]],
s4:[["Nobody will punish you for eating noodles with your hands at a wedding dinner, but people will stare.",["Folkways","Mores","Laws","Values"],"Folkways are everyday customs. Breaking them brings only mild disapproval."],
 ["A toddler learns to say “thank you” at home, learns to queue at school, then picks up slang from friends.",["Socialisation through family, school and peers","Cultural lag","Social stratification","Formal social control"],"Socialisation is lifelong learning of norms, and each agency teaches a different part."],
 ["Grandparents own smartphones, but they still do not trust online payments.",["Cultural lag","Folkways","Sanskritisation","Socialisation"],"Ogburn’s cultural lag: material culture, the device, changes faster than non-material culture, the habits and beliefs."]],
s5:[["Two rival coaching centres fight for students with bigger and bigger hoardings, but neither breaks the law.",["Competition","Conflict","Cooperation","Assimilation"],"Competition is an impersonal struggle for a scarce goal within accepted rules. Conflict aims at the opponent directly."],
 ["A calm queue outside a stadium hears that tickets are over. Within minutes people are pushing and breaking barriers.",["A crowd turning into a mob","Assimilation","Cooperation","A primary group"],"A mob is an emotionally charged, active crowd in which individuals lose their usual restraint."]],
s6:[["Before surveying your classmates, you write: “Students who sleep less score lower.”",["A hypothesis","A sample","An observation","A report"],"A hypothesis is a tentative statement that the research will test."],
 ["To study a music festival, a researcher buys a ticket, joins the crowd and takes notes for three days.",["Participant observation","Questionnaire","Structured interview","Random sampling"],"The researcher becomes part of the group being studied to see it from the inside."]],
s7:[["In the village everyone knows your grandfather’s name. In the city flat you have never spoken to the family next door.",["Urban anonymity","Joint family","Sanskritisation","Rural indebtedness"],"Village relations are close and face-to-face. City life is impersonal, with weak neighbourhood ties."],
 ["A family of six lives in one room beside a railway line and shares one tap with the lane, because city rents are too high.",["Slums as an urban problem","Rural indebtedness","Urban anonymity","Caste panchayat"],"Migration and costly housing push the urban poor into overcrowded settlements without basic services."]],
s8:[["A ten-year-old serves tea at a stall all day while children his age walk past to school.",["Child labour","Juvenile delinquency","Disguised unemployment","Population explosion"],"Work that takes away a child’s schooling and childhood is child labour, and poverty is its main cause."],
 ["A country has so many people aged 15 to 35 that, with skills and jobs, it could grow very fast.",["Demographic dividend","Population explosion","Juvenile delinquency","Cultural lag"],"A large working-age share is an opportunity, but only if the young are educated and employed."]]
};
var ECV={more:null,busy:false};
function ecSt(){S.ec=S.ec||{};S.ec.d=S.ec.d||{};return S.ec}
function ecChs(sid){return scbOf(sid).filter(function(c){return EC[c.id]})}
function ecCount(sid){var d=ecSt().d,n=0,t=0;ecChs(sid).forEach(function(c){EC[c.id].forEach(function(x,i){t++;if(d[c.id+'_'+i])n++})});return{n:n,t:t}}
function ecShuf(a){a=a.slice();for(var i=a.length-1;i>0;i--){var j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1)),t=a[i];a[i]=a[j];a[j]=t}return a}
function ecOpen(sid,ch){var d=ecSt().d,q=null;
  if(ch){var all=[];(ch==='*'?ecChs(sid):[cbCh(ch)]).forEach(function(c){EC[c.id].forEach(function(x,i){all.push({c:c.id,i:i})})});
    if(ch==='*'){var un=ecShuf(all.filter(function(k){return!d[k.c+'_'+k.i]}));q=(un.length?un:ecShuf(all)).slice(0,8)}
    else q=all.filter(function(k){return!d[k.c+'_'+k.i]}).concat(all.filter(function(k){return d[k.c+'_'+k.i]}))}
  SJV.ec={sid:sid,q:q,pos:0,pick:null,ok:0,o:null};SJV.ta=null;SJV.p=null;SJV.rv=null;SJV.qz=null;SJV.mm=null;ECV={more:null,busy:false};view='sj_'+sid;render();window.scrollTo(0,0)}
function ecView(){var V=SJV.ec,s=sjSub(V.sid);if(V.q)return ecCase();
  var d=ecSt().d,T=ecCount(V.sid);
  var h='<p class="back"><button class="link" onclick="SJV.ec=null;render();window.scrollTo(0,0)">← '+esc(s.n)+'</button></p><h1>Everyday cases</h1><p class="sub">Short scenes from ordinary life: cricket, music, school, family, the phone in your hand. Read the scene and spot the idea from '+esc(s.n)+' at work in it.</p>';
  h+='<div class="hprog"><div class="bar"><i style="width:'+Math.round(T.n/T.t*100)+'%;background:var(--c4)"></i></div><span>'+T.n+' of '+T.t+' spotted</span></div>';
  h+='<button class="btn" style="text-align:center;background:var(--c4);color:var(--card);margin:0 0 14px" onclick="ecOpen(\''+V.sid+'\',\'*\')">Play a mixed round</button>';
  h+='<div class="card"><b>Or choose a chapter</b>'+ecChs(V.sid).map(function(c){var t=EC[c.id].length,n=EC[c.id].filter(function(x,i){return d[c.id+'_'+i]}).length;
    return'<button class="ecrow" onclick="ecOpen(\''+V.sid+'\',\''+c.id+'\')"><b>'+esc(c.n)+'</b>'+(n===t?'<span class="pill ok">All spotted</span>':'<span class="pill">'+n+' of '+t+'</span>')+'<span class="bar"><i style="width:'+Math.round(n/t*100)+'%"></i></span></button>'}).join('')+'</div>';
  app.innerHTML=h}
function ecCase(){var V=SJV.ec,s=sjSub(V.sid),hasC=typeof claude!=='undefined'&&claude&&claude.use;
  var back='<p class="back"><button class="link" onclick="SJV.ec.q=null;render();window.scrollTo(0,0)">← Everyday cases</button></p>';
  if(V.pos>=V.q.length){var T=ecCount(V.sid);
    app.innerHTML=back+'<div class="card ecend" style="text-align:center"><div class="eyebrow">Round finished</div><b>'+V.ok+' of '+V.q.length+'</b><p class="sub" style="margin:4px 0 10px">spotted on the first try. '+T.n+' of '+T.t+' cases in '+esc(s.n)+' are done.</p><button class="btn" style="text-align:center" onclick="ecOpen(\''+V.sid+'\',\'*\')">Play another round</button><button class="btn alt" style="text-align:center" onclick="SJV.ec.q=null;render()">Choose a chapter</button></div>';return}
  var k=V.q[V.pos],x=EC[k.c][k.i],ch=cbCh(k.c);if(!V.o)V.o=ecShuf([0,1,2,3]);var done=V.pick!=null,right=done&&V.o[V.pick]===0;
  var h=back+'<div class="meta"><span>Case '+(V.pos+1)+' of '+V.q.length+'</span><span>'+esc(ch.n)+'</span></div><div class="bar"><i style="width:'+Math.round(V.pos/V.q.length*100)+'%;background:var(--c4)"></i></div>';
  h+='<div class="card"><p class="ecscene">'+esc(x[0])+'</p></div><p class="ecq">Which idea is at work here?</p>';
  h+=V.o.map(function(oi,j){return'<button class="opt'+(done?(oi===0?' ok':j===V.pick?' bad':''):'')+'"'+(done?' disabled':'')+' onclick="ecPick('+j+')">'+esc(x[1][oi])+'</button>'}).join('');
  if(done){h+='<div class="fb '+(right?'ok':'bad')+'"><b>'+(right?'Spotted.':'It was '+esc(x[1][0])+'.')+'</b> '+esc(x[2])+'</div>';
    if(hasC)h+='<button class="btn alt" style="text-align:center" id="ecMoreB" onclick="ecMore()">✨ Show me this idea somewhere else</button><div id="ecMore">'+(ECV.more?'<div class="claude" style="margin-top:8px">'+esc(ECV.more)+'</div>':'')+'</div>';
    h+='<button class="btn" style="text-align:center" onclick="ecNext()">'+(V.pos+1<V.q.length?'Next case →':'Finish the round')+'</button>'}
  app.innerHTML=h}
function ecPick(j){var V=SJV.ec;if(!V||V.pick!=null)return;V.pick=j;var k=V.q[V.pos];if(V.o[j]===0){V.ok++;ecSt().d[k.c+'_'+k.i]=Date.now();save()}var y=window.scrollY;render();window.scrollTo(0,y)}
function ecNext(){var V=SJV.ec;V.pos++;V.pick=null;V.o=null;ECV.more=null;render();window.scrollTo(0,0)}
async function ecMore(){var V=SJV.ec;if(!V||!V.q||ECV.busy)return;var k=V.q[V.pos],x=EC[k.c][k.i],o=document.getElementById('ecMore'),b=document.getElementById('ecMoreB');ECV.busy=true;if(b)b.disabled=true;if(o)o.innerHTML='<p class="sub" style="margin:8px 0 0">Claude is thinking of another one…</p>';
  try{var s=await getS();if(!s)throw new Error('none');
    var d=await s.json('A senior-school student is learning '+sjSub(V.sid).n+' through everyday examples. Give ONE fresh everyday scene that shows the same idea, different from the one already seen. Draw it from ordinary teenage life'+(S.int?' or from these interests: '+S.int:'')+'. Two or three sentences for the scene, then one sentence saying how the idea shows up in it. Keep the idea accurate. Use neutral wording for the student and never assume their gender, class or school. Plain text only, no markdown, at most 80 words.\nIDEA: '+x[1][0]+'\nWHAT IT MEANS: '+x[2]+'\nSCENE ALREADY SEEN: '+x[0]+(ECV.more?'\nALSO ALREADY SEEN: '+ECV.more:'')+'\nReturn JSON only: {"example": "the new scene and the link to the idea"}',{cache:false,modelTier:'default'});
    if(!d||!d.example)throw new Error('shape');ECV.more=String(d.example);if(o)o.innerHTML='<div class="claude" style="margin-top:8px">'+esc(ECV.more)+'</div>'}
  catch(e){if(o)o.innerHTML='<p class="sub" style="margin:8px 0 0">'+(e&&e.code?sampleErr(e):'Claude couldn’t think of one just now. Try again in a moment.')+'</p>'}
  ECV.busy=false;var b2=document.getElementById('ecMoreB');if(b2){b2.disabled=false;b2.textContent='✨ Show me another one'}}
